id: rule-whilet-decreasing
title: A decreasing variant
topic: Loop
example-of: WHILE-TOTAL
derived: true

task:
  Use the rule WHILE-TOTAL to show that the loop terminates, with the
  invariant $i <= 10$ and the variant $E = 10 - i$. The logical variable
  $n$ is the value of $E$ when an iteration starts.

given:
  Loop condition B: $i < 10$
  Variant E: $10 - i$
  n: the value of $E$ when an iteration starts

pre: i <= 10
post: i <= 10 && !(i < 10)

scheme: WHILE-TOTAL
  Preservation: {«I» && «i < 10 : B» && «10 - i = n : E = n»} i := i + 1 {«I» && «10 - i < n : E < n»}
  Variant: «I» && «i < 10 : B» ==> «10 - i >= 0 : E ≥ 0»
  ---
  {«I»} while i < 10 do i := i + 1 {«I» && «!(i < 10) : ¬B»}

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while i < 10 do {
    {I && i < 10 && 10 - i = n}
    {R1}
    i := i + 1     // b
    {I && 10 - i < n}
  }
  {I && !(i < 10)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: while
text:
  The rule uses the precondition of its conclusion as the invariant.
set: I = i <= 10

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, I
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: i <= 10 ==> i <= 10
why:
  $P$ and $I$ are the same formula.

step: The variant is not negative
part: Variant
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: I && i < 10 && 10 - i = n
text:
  When an iteration starts, the variant must not be negative.
  >>> «I» && «i < 10 : B» ==> «10 - i >= 0 : E ≥ 0»
  We simplify the right side.
calc:
  10 - i >= 0
  = i <= 10      // add i to both sides
vc: i <= 10 && i < 10 ==> 10 - i >= 0
why:
  The right side is $I$ itself.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the body
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN
focus: b
text:
  One iteration must keep $I$ and make the variant smaller than $n$:
  >>> {«I» && «i < 10 : B» && «10 - i = n : E = n»} i := i + 1 {«I» && «10 - i < n : E < n»}
  We substitute the variable $i$ with $i + 1$ in the postcondition of the
  body.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, i <= 10 && 10 - i < n)
  = (i <= 10 && 10 - i < n)[i <- i + 1]
  = i + 1 <= 10 && 10 - (i + 1) < n      // i replaced by i + 1
  = i < 10 && 10 - (i + 1) < n           // i + 1 <= 10 is i < 10, for integers
  = i < 10 && 9 - i < n                  // 10 - (i + 1) = 9 - i
set: R1 = i < 10 && 9 - i < n

step: The precondition of the body implies R1
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && i < 10 && 10 - i = n, R1
text:
  >>> «I» && «i < 10 : B» && «10 - i = n : E = n» ==> «R1»
vc: i <= 10 && i < 10 && 10 - i = n ==> i < 10 && 9 - i < n
why:
  The conjunct $i < 10$ is $B$. From $10 - i = n$ we get $9 - i = n - 1$,
  which is smaller than $n$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && !(i < 10), Q
text:
  >>> «I» && «!(i < 10) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: i <= 10 && !(i < 10) ==> i <= 10 && !(i < 10)
why:
  The two formulas are the same: $Q$ is $I && !B$. All premises hold, so
  the loop terminates.
